 is 2 left, so T2-T3-T4 chow possible, but next player\u2019s history doesn\u2019t show T focus; across player has no T meld; previous player has T meld (T6-T7-T8). Still, T3 less useful for me.  \n\nBut if I aim for All Types pattern (6 pts), I need to keep one of each suit + wind + dragon. Currently I have all five types; discarding B1 or B5 loses B suit; discarding T3 loses T suit. For All Types, I must keep at least one tile of T and B until the end. If I discard one suit now, I can still draw it back later, but risky.  \n\nGiven early/mid game (many discards already), I\u2019ll focus on **Half Flush** because All Types still needs other melds which I don\u2019t have yet.  \n\nSo priority: keep W and honors (F3, J2), discard others.  \n\nBest discard now: **B1** \u2014 less useful, minimal risk.  \n\n---\n\n**Reason:**  \nB1 is not part of my planned Half Flush (W + honors) strategy, and with only 2 B1 left in wall, it\u2019s unlikely to complete a useful meld for me. It maintains flexibility to keep All Types possibility later if I draw another B, but right now simplifying toward a clear scoring pattern is more important. Among non-W, non-honors, B1 is the most isolated and safe to discard given other players\u2019 visible melds.\n\n---\n\n**Answer:** Play B1"}
